40
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
But more importantly, I would love to have you connect with the implications

41
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
of following and serving the king of the universe.

42
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Does that sound fair?

43
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Which leads me into the reminder of our three categories that we're using

44
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,000
when covering these, when working through these sermons.

45
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
One is the surprising arrival of the kingdom.

46
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
How the kingdom comes in unexpected ways.

47
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Remember, if you think back to my couple of sermons ago when I preached,

48
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
remember, I want you to do the work of building a theology of the kingdom.

49
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Use the Scriptures, talk to the spirit, use books, use community,

50
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and begin to uncover what it looks like to be a follower of Jesus

51
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
and living in the kingdom of God.

52
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Two, the surprising ethic of the kingdom of God.

53
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
We're going to talk about this more today.

54
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,000
But with building a kingdom theology comes kingdom ethic.

55
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
What does Jesus want his followers to do?

56
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
How does Jesus want his followers to act, or I would even say react?

57
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Did Jesus go to the cross, rise three days later, and ascend to the throne of heaven

58
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
so that you would reduce your faith?

59
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
And I'm going to say this jokingly, but so that we would reduce our faith

60
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
to avoiding bad words and dressing up once a week.

61
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
I say that jokingly, but what is your kingdom ethic?

62
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Three, the surprise implications of the kingdom of God.

63
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
What are you going to do now with this information as it's been presented to you?

64
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
That's why I want you in community.

65
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
What does that look like?

66
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
And I'm going to set up our sermon today with a question.

67
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Are we familiar with the phrase, hurt people, hurt people?

68
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,000
I don't know where I heard it before.

69
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
I don't know if you've heard it before.

70
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Well, it sounds like a lot of you have.

71
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
And if you haven't heard it before, I expect royalties and quotation marks

72
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,000
every time you use it now.

73
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
You use it in slides, put my name on it.

74
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
No, but I don't remember where I heard it, but I do remember how it hit me.

75
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
I'm like, it was a light bulb moment, right?

76
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Like, whoa, that's wise.

77
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Because we know, whether from observation or personal experience,

78
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:44,000
that most, not all, but most, people end up hurting others

79
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
in a reaction to their own pain.

80
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
It suggests that when someone carries an unresolved hurt,

81
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
when they don't confront it, they can't name it,

82
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
or they refuse to heal from it,

83
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
it doesn't just sit quietly in the corner of the room.

84
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,000
It leaks out.

85
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,000
It will lash out.

86
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
And someone else always gets caught in the spill.

87
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Are we tracking?

88
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Now, don't worry.

89
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
We're not going to go down a full field or psychological rabbit hole this morning.

90
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
What YouTube's for.

91
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
I kid.

92
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
But I share that phrase because it sets framework

93
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
for something we're going to talk about today.

94
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Here's the shift.

95
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
If in the world, and I would even go as far as to say in the world's kingdom,

96
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
hurt people hurt people,

97
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
then in the kingdom of heaven,

98
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
the kind of world that Jesus came to restore,

99
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
the opposite must be true.

100
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Forgiven people forgive people.

101
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
And that's where we're going today.

102
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,000
So let's pray before we get into our text.

103
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
So keep that in the forefront of your minds.

104
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:52,000
But let's pray.

105
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Dear Heavenly Father,

106
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,000
I come to you today as an intercessor for your people, Lord.

107
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Those here in the room and those listening online,

108
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Lord, maybe they haven't prayed this yet.

109
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Maybe I haven't prayed this for myself.

110
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
But I ask it now to soften our hearts.

111
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Soften our hearts to hear your Holy Spirit.

112
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Open our eyes to the ways we live that are not of you.

113
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Especially the ways we've unknowingly been discipled by the world around us.

114
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Give us the courage to live in your ways, Lord.

115
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Even when they're hard.

116
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Even when they cost us something.

117
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Especially when they cost us something.

118
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
I hear the words of Peter.

119
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Lord, to whom else shall we go?

120
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
You alone have the words of eternal life.

121
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Lord, as I speak today, I pray that no one hears my voice.

122
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Only yours within it.

123
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
I pray that no one sees me.

124
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,000
They only see you through me.

125
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
We pray all this for your glory and our neighbor's good.

126
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Amen.

127
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:59,000
The text we're going to look at today is Matthew 18:21-35.

128
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
And here's the game plan.

129
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,000
Here's the rundown.

130
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
We're going to read that section.

131
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
And I'm going to give you some homework.

132
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
But we're going to read that together.

133
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,000
We're going to set it in context.

134
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Then we're going to come back and unpack it.

135
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
We're going to be in the CSB this morning.

136
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
So if those of you that brought your Bibles and you want to follow along.

137
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:17,000
But it's going to be up on the screen as well.

138
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
We're going to read that.

139
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,000
But I want you to go back and read the entirety of Matthew 18.

140
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
I did this alone by myself this morning.

141
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:32,000
And that's not a, I often forget my alone time with the Lord.

142
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
So don't hear that as like, wow, of course it's spiritual.

143
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:46,000
But I read that this morning slowly and meditatively that there's a huge amount of information in Matthew 18 that I think is very relevant to the church today.

144
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:52,000
So I would challenge you to spend some time with the Lord this week and read Matthew 18.

145
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,000
But I'm going to give you some clues and some tools as we do that.

146
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:01,000
So together, let's read Matthew 18, 21 through 35 this morning.

147
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:13,000
Then Peter approached him and asked, Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother or sister who sins against me?

148
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
As many as seven times?

149
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
I tell you, not as many as seven, Jesus replied, but 70 times seven.

150
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:32,000
For this reason, the kingdom of heaven can be compared to a king who wants to settle, that wanted to settle accounts with his servants.

151
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:39,000
When he began to settle accounts, one who owed 10,000 talents was brought before him.

152
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Since he did not have the money to pay it back, his master commanded that he, his wife and his children and everything he had be sold to pay the debt.

153
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:58,000
At this, the servant fell face down before him and said, be patient with me and I will pay you everything.

154
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Then the master of the servant had compassion and released him and forgave the loan.

155
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:10,000
That servant went out and found one of his fellow servants who owed him 100 denarii.

156
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:16,000
He grabbed him, started choking him and say, pay what you owe.

157
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:25,000
At this, his fellow servant fell down and began begging him, be patient with me and I will pay you back.

158
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,000
But he wasn't willing.

159
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Instead, he went and threw him into prison until he can pay what was owed.

160
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:38,000
When the servant saw what had taken place, they were deeply distressed and went and reported to their master everything that had happened.

161
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:49,000
Then after he summoned him, his master said to him, you wicked servant, I forgave you all the debt because you begged me.

162
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Shouldn't you have also had mercy on your fellow servant?

163
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
As I have had mercy on you.

164
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:06,000
And because he was angry, his master handed him over to the jailers to be tortured until he could pay everything that was owed.

165
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:15,000
So also my heavenly father will do to you unless every one of you forgives his brother or sister from your heart.

166
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:20,000
I told Donovan, I was joking with him.

167
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:21,000
He's like, are you nervous?

168
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
And I'm like, no, because Jesus said all the hard words and I don't have to.

169
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
So let's talk context.

170
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And with that, I want us to zoom out into the entirety of the chapter.

171
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:41,000
And we're going to start by defining some terms about what is going on right before Jesus drops this parable in response to Peter.

172
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Now I hold loosely and I say loosely, not because I'm like, well, I'm not really there.

173
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
I hold loosely because I want the words of Jesus to conform my thinking.

174
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,000
But I hold loosely to what's called a two kingdoms theology.

175
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:03,000
It's an Anabaptist perspective, not anti-Baptist, Ana-Baptist perspective that sees two competing power systems.

176
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,000
We've got a slide for it.

177
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:10,000
The kingdom of this world that's built on control, hierarchy, prestige, and competition.

178
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,000
Are we aware of this kingdom?

179
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
We're aware of this kingdom.

180
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:18,000
And the kingdom of heaven that is marked by humility, service, and forgiveness.

181
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
These kingdoms aren't compatible.

182
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
In fact, I would say they're at odds.

183
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:30,000
As followers of Jesus, we're called out of the first and into the second.

184
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,000
The apostle Paul says this in 2 Corinthians 5:20.

185
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:41,000
We are ambassadors for Christ since God is making his appeal through us.

186
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Ambassadors, if you don't know, live in one kingdom.

187
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
I live here.

188
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
But embody the kingdom of another.

189
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
So do we.

190
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:52,000
We.

191
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:53,000
We live here.

192
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
But we embody the kingdom of God.

193
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,000
One that I would argue is upside down by worldly standards.

194
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:04,000
So when the disciples asked Jesus, who is the greatest in the beginning of Matthew 18?

195
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?

196
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
Are they working with what category?

197
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
The worlds, right?

198
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,000
Who's on top?

199
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,000
Who wins?

200
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Who has the most power?

201
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Who has the most prestige?

202
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
How does Jesus respond to this?

203
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:29,000
He responds by calling a child from the crowd to the center saying, whoever humbles himself like this child is the greatest.

204
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Folks, I view this as kingdom reversal.

205
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:45,000
This is Jesus setting up what his kingdom is, what it is not, and what his kingdom does to restore what creation was supposed to be, not affirm what it has become.

206
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:51,000
So if you hang around with me, you'll probably hear me use these terms.

207
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
In the kingdom of the world, power is over.

208
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,000
The kingdom of God, power is under.

209
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
What do you mean by that?

210
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Greatness isn't about rising up, but stooping low.

211
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:07,000
Not dominance, but dependence.

212
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:12,000
This is for me, not climbing higher, but kneeling lower.

213
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
When we say that, I'm going to go on my ad-lib a little bit here.

214
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:26,000
If you're not familiar with the posture of kneeling, it is awkward to us Americans.

215
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:32,000
But there is something powerful about kneeling lower.

216
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Jesus is preparing the disciples for something they don't fully grasp yet.

217
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:47,000
That the systems of power that they've always known, built on hierarchy, vengeance, and retribution, are about to be undone at Calvary.

218
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:59,000
Through his death, resurrection, and ascension to the throne of heaven, Jesus replaces the systems of power and replaces them with a kingdom built on sacrificial love.

219
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:07,000
Greatness is redefined as servanthood, authority reimagined as a humble, self-giving love.

220
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
Now this echo, this echoes throughout the Gospels.

221
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:17,000
There would not have been a time a master would have kneeled to wash his disciples' feet.

222
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:24,000
When a king rode into the capital city, he did not do it on a donkey.

223
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Jesus subverts the worldly expectations because his kingdom is not like the kingdoms of this world.

224
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:38,000
So with that framework in mind, we come to Peter's question in verse 21.

225
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:43,000
Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother or sister?

226
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
As many as seven times?

227
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
And that sounds generous.

228
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
That sounded generous by their standards.

229
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:56,000
It even sounds generous to our standards, and we don't have rabbinical teachings that we stick to.

230
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:02,000
But most first century rabbis would have taught the three times forgiving your brother or sister was sufficient.

231
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,000
So Peter is more than doubling that.

232
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
He thinks he's being kingdom minded.

233
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:13,000
But Jesus replies, not seven times, but 70 times seven.

234
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:19,000
In other words, Jesus is saying, what you are doing is trying to count forgiveness.

235
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
I'm calling you to be people of forgiveness.

236
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
Forgiveness isn't about keeping score.

237
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
It's not a math problem or a formula.

238
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
For kingdom people, it's a way of life.

239
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:37,000
Because the kingdom of heaven, forgiven people, forgive people.

240
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:42,000
Now we turn to the parable and the power of grace.

241
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Jesus isn't just giving Peter a surprising answer.

242
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:51,000
He's inviting him into a radically different economy of mercy.

243
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
And I want to make sure we don't miss it.

244
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:00,000
He tells a story that confronts, that makes, that forces us to confront what kind of kingdom we are actually living in.

245
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:06,000
A parable of a servant who is forgiven an unpayable debt that refuses to give a peer a much smaller one.

246
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Now I know we don't work in talents, but even some of your Bible translations may render it this way.

247
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:18,000
But 10,000 talents was a massive sum.

248
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Records in 2 Chronicles, thank you, talk about that the temple cost 8,000 talents.

249
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:34,000
That this is an unimaginable debt for anyone's servant to possess.

250
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
It's unrealistic, so keep that in mind.

251
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:45,000
A parable of a servant who is forgiven an unpayable debt, then refuses to forgive a peer, a much smaller one.

252
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
It's a clash of kingdoms.

253
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:53,000
The world system of accounting, revenge and retribution,

254
00:17:53,000 --> 00:18:00,000
versus the kingdom of heaven where forgiveness flows from the bottomless mercy of a gracious king.

255
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:06,000
And before we walk through it again, I want to define another word, grace.

256
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:11,000
I think we use this word in our Christianese.

257
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:15,000
That means a lot of things to a lot of people.

258
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:16,000
This is how I'm using it.

259
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:24,000
In Jewish Scripture, when we read grace in our Old Testament, the Hebrew word is chased.

260
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:33,000
Scholars argue about it, which defines God's covenantal love, steadfast mercy, and his faithfulness.

261
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:46,000
But in the first century Greco-Roman world, which Jesus is navigating, the word chares, or grace, as we see it in our New Testament, carries a strong relational expectation.

262
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
Grace isn't just a one-sided gift.

263
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:50,000
Grace isn't just a one-sided gift.

264
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:51,000
It was part of a social dynamic.

265
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:59,000
A patron, a king, someone in power, would extend a favor or resource to a client, someone under him.

266
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:06,000
And the client, in turn, was expected to respond with gratitude, loyalty, and a transformed relationship.

267
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:10,000
Grace created relational obligation.

268
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:16,000
Not repayment, but alignment, loyalty, if you will.

269
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:20,000
You live differently because the grace you received.

270
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:24,000
And the grace reflected the loyalty of the master who gave it.

271
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,000
Does that sound familiar?

272
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:32,000
So when Jesus talks about grace, mercy, and forgiveness, he's not just describing God's generosity.

273
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:36,000
He's describing a transformative call to the recipient of grace.

274
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
You have received grace.

275
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Now live as one who belongs to the gracious king.

276
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:47,000
To receive grace and not extend it, that's not just bad manners.

277
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,000
And like Sam said last week, it's still kind of cringy in our culture.

278
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:56,000
But in first century, it's rebellion against the king's very nature.

279
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:00,000
So in this parable, Jesus isn't just making a moral point.

280
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,000
He's revealing the spiritual logic of the kingdom.

281
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:09,000
You, us, we, have been forgiven a debt we could never repay.

282
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
Those within the church.

283
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:17,000
You, those within the church, have not been met with punishment, but with mercy.

284
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:24,000
You, church, are now a carrier of that mercy and called to extend it to others.

285
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:30,000
Because in the kingdom of heaven, forgiven people forgive people.

286
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:35,000
That's just not a slogan or a bumper sticker to slap on your car or a moral takeaway.

287
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:38,000
It's a kingdom reality, I think, from this parable.

288
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Jesus' parable, the unforgiving servant, paints a vivid picture of two competing kingdoms.

289
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:46,000
One, where grace flows freely and mercy defines power.

290
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:53,000
The other one, where debts are tallied, power is hoarded and forgiveness is foreign.

291
00:20:55,000 --> 00:21:00,000
Now that we've set the stage, understanding the two kingdoms and what it means to live as foreign people,

292
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
let's zoom back in on the parable itself.

293
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:07,000
As we walk through this story again, watch closely to how the characters behave.

294
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
How the king acts.

295
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
How the servant responds.

296
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:15,000
Think of that clash of two realities.

297
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,000
One, where mercy is abundant and debts are wiped clean.

298
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:25,000
The other where debts are counted, grudges are held tight and forgiveness is withheld.

299
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:29,000
Let's look carefully at the first scene of the story, the unimaginable debt.

300
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:33,000
What does it say about who we are before God?

301
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:38,000
What does it mean that the king forgave the debt entirely?

302
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:44,000
Then we'll see the stark contrast as the same servant refuses to extend mercy to someone else.

303
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:48,000
That refusal isn't just about money or fairness, right?

304
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Let's take even the money off the table.

305
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:55,000
It's a sign of which kingdom's values are truly ruling your heart.

306
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:02,000
So with that, let's walk through the story step by step and listen for what the king has to say to us today.

307
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
Start with verses 23 through 27.

308
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:13,000
For this reason, the kingdom of heaven can be compared to a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.

309
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:21,000
When he began to settle accounts, one who owed him 10,000 talents, remember, think of a large amount, was brought before him.

310
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:29,000
Since he did not have the money to pay it back, his master commanded that he, his wife, his children, and everything he had be sold to pay the debt.

311
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:37,000
At this, the servant fell face down before him and said, be patient with me and I will pay you everything.

312
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:46,860
Then the master of the house, then the master of that servant had compassion, released him and forgave him the loan.

313
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:50,000
And this is where Jesus grabs our attention, right?

314
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
And I think we've all talked about this.

315
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
Find the problem, right?

316
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
No one, and we talked about this a little bit ago.

317
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:59,000
Nobody could have amassed this amount of debt.

318
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:02,000
This is where Jesus is grasping the crowd.

319
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
This is an absurd moment.

320
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
Think millions in today's money.

321
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:10,000
It's like Jesus is saying, this guy owed a portion of the national debt.

322
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
No one would have Lent him this much money.

323
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,000
So everyone listening to this parable would have kind of chuckled, right?

324
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
They would have been like, nobody can do that.

325
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:23,000
Because no one could owe that much, let alone pay it back.

326
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
It's kind of the point of the parable.

327
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:28,000
This, I would say, is us before God.

328
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
We don't owe just a little.

329
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
We're spiritually bankrupt people.

330
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
We have no leverage.

331
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:37,000
We have no way to make it right on our own.

332
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:41,000
But still we plead, be patient, and I'll pay it back.

333
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
But we can't.

334
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
The math does not work out.

335
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
And here's the beauty of the story.

336
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
The king knows that.

337
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
He doesn't restructure the loan.

338
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:53,000
He doesn't give him more time.

339
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:54,000
What's he do?

340
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
He cancels the debt.

341
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:01,000
Releasing him, not just from prison, but from the crushing weight of his failure.

342
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:04,000
And the money he's borrowed that he could not pay back.

343
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
That's grace.

344
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
That's the recipient, being a recipient of grace.

345
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
That is the kingdom.

346
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
And notice the king's motivation.

347
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
Compassion.

348
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:21,000
Not obligation, not pity, but compassion.

349
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:24,000
The king doesn't say, "Fine, I'll let you off this time."

350
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
You got six more times.

351
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:27,000
That's it.

352
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
After six, no more.

353
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
No, he's moved.

354
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:36,000
His mercy flows from who he is, not what the servant deserves.

355
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:41,000
In this first scene, we see the heartbeat of the kingdom of heaven.

356
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:45,000
A compassionate king willing to absorb the cost and set the guilty free.

357
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:48,000
What can wash away my sins?

358
00:24:50,000 --> 00:25:01,000
And the implied question then is, if this is how our king treats us, how then will you treat others?

359
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
Let's keep reading.

360
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:04,000
Verses 28 through 30.

361
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:09,000
That servant went out and found one of his fellow servants who owed him a hundred denarii.

362
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:12,000
Think, a couple thousand dollars.

363
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:18,000
He grabbed him, started choking him, and said, "Pay me what you owe."

364
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:26,000
At this, his fellow servant fell down and began begging him, "Be patient with me, and I will pay you back."

365
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
But he wasn't willing.

366
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:34,000
Instead, he went and threw him into prison until he can pay what was owed.

367
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:42,000
If the first scene felt unbelievable in the king's generosity, the second scene is shocking in its cruelty.

368
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:51,000
This man who just walked away from the king's throne completely debt-free goes out and chokes a guy over a couple months' wages.

369
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:55,000
The language is violent.

370
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:57,000
I think it's on purpose.

371
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:00,000
It's meant to disgust us.

372
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:09,000
Because this is a picture of what happens when the world's kingdom still rules in our hearts, even after we've tasted the grace of heaven.

373
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:13,000
Here's the tragedy.

374
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:18,000
The second servant uses the exact same words the first one did.

375
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:22,000
"Be patient with me, and I will pay you back."

376
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:25,000
Deja vu.

377
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
But this time, there is no mercy.

378
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:28,000
Right?

379
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:35,000
The first time the servant uses this phrase to the master, the master is moved to compassion and forgives the debt.

380
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,000
And not just a debt, right?

381
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:38,000
Remember, an insurmountable debt.

382
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
Not just a few months' wages here.

383
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:49,000
And the second time the phrase is used, from the servant to the fellow servant, it is met with contempt and violence.

384
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,000
Why?

385
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:58,000
Because this man received mercy from the master, but he didn't let it transform him.

386
00:26:58,000 --> 00:26:59,000
And he didn't extend it.

387
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:07,000
Meaning, that he didn't receive the grace at all, because grace is always reciprocated.

388
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:11,000
He was forgiven, but not changed.

389
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:15,000
He was released, but not renewed.

390
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:17,000
And let's be honest.

391
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:20,000
Hopefully, this should hit close to home.

392
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:26,000
We've all received mercy from God, and then withheld it from others.

393
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:30,000
Is that grace?

394
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
No.

395
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:46,000
We've all at some point choked someone emotionally, held a grudge, clung to bitterness, demanded payment in the form of an apology, performance, or punishment.

396
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
Why?

397
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:53,000
I think we're still learning to live in the right kingdom.

398
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:58,000
In the kingdom of heaven, forgiven people forgive people.

399
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:04,000
But only when they really understand what forgiveness costs.

400
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
And what it really means.

401
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:10,000
Let's finish this parable, verses 31 through 35.

402
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:18,000
When the other servants saw what had taken place, they were deeply distressed, and went and reported to their master everything that had happened.

403
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:24,000
Then, after he had summoned him, his master said to him, "You wicked servant.

404
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:27,000
I forgave you all the debt because you begged me.

405
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:34,000
Shouldn't you have also had mercy on your fellow servant?

406
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:38,000
As I have had mercy on you."

407
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:46,000
And because he was angry, his master handed him over to the jailers to be tortured until he could pay everything that was owed.

408
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:56,000
So also, my heavenly Father will do to you unless every one of you forgives his brother or sister from your heart.

409
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:59,000
It's heavy.

410
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:01,000
Right?

411
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:02,000
Let's...

412
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
I'm not going to try to soften Jesus' words there.

413
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
This is meant to shock us.

414
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:09,000
It's supposed to make us uncomfortable.

415
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
Because I think the point is clear.

416
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:20,000
There is no room in God's kingdom for people who demand mercy for themselves but refuse to show it to others.

417
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:27,000
Jesus isn't saying God's forgiveness is earned by our ability to forgive.

418
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
If you are hearing that, you are mishearing me.

419
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:39,000
He's saying if we've truly received mercy, it will shape how we live and how we extend mercy.

420
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:44,000
And if it doesn't, then we really haven't let it in.

421
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:47,000
We've visited the king's courtroom.

422
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,000
We've had a conversation with the king.

423
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:54,000
But we're still unwilling to live under the laws of the king.

424
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:57,000
We'd rather live in the laws of the old kingdom.

425
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:00,000
Jesus ends with a sobering line.

426
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:06,000
Unless every one of you forgives his brother or sister from your heart.

427
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:09,000
Not just words.

428
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,000
Not just politeness.

429
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:15,000
From your heart.

430
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:18,000
Not fake peace.

431
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,000
Not pretending like nothing happened.

432
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:27,000
But instead a deep release rooted in your own experience of grace.

433
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:32,000
I'm going to let that sit with you.

434
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:33,000
And I'll ask you.

435
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:37,000
What kingdom are you living in?

436
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:40,000
Or whose kingdom are you living in?

437
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:43,000
The kingdom of keeping score?

438
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:47,000
Of demanding justice on your terms?

439
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:51,000
Of holding people hostage until they make it right?

440
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:56,000
Or the kingdom of grace?

441
00:30:56,000 --> 00:30:58,000
Where mercy flows?

442
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:00,000
And this is important.

443
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:02,000
Not because others deserve it.

444
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:05,000
But because Lord knows we didn't.

445
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:10,000
And God forgives us anyway.

446
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:14,000
In the kingdom of heaven, forgiven people forgive people.

447
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:16,000
Not perfectly.

448
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:17,000
Not easily.

449
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:19,000
But progressively.

450
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:23,000
As the Holy Spirit keeps reminding us of how much we've been forgiven.

451
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:25,000
So let me ask you today.

452
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:29,000
I want you to sit with this.

453
00:31:29,000 --> 00:31:31,000
Who are you choking in your heart?

454
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:36,000
What debts are you still trying to collect?

455
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:42,000
Where is God calling you to live as a citizen of his kingdom?

456
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:45,000
Maybe for you.

457
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:46,000
Maybe for you.

458
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:47,000
It's time to cancel a debt.

459
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:50,000
To forgive someone who never said sorry.

460
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:53,000
This is me.

461
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:54,000
I'm guilty of this.

462
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:57,000
To stop rehearsing the offense and release them to the grace of God.

463
00:31:57,000 --> 00:32:00,000
Not because they deserve it.

464
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:03,000
But because you didn't deserve it either.

465
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:05,000
And that's true.

466
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:06,000
And if that's true.

467
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:07,000
And I believe it is.

468
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:11,000
Then how could we possibly hold tight-fisted unforgiveness towards others?

469
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:14,000
That's the tragedy of the parable.

470
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:18,000
That the first servant is shown extraordinary mercy.

471
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:22,000
Millions of dollars worth of debt canceled immediately.

472
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:24,000
A lifetime of failure wiped clean.

473
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:28,000
But instead of being transformed by that mercy.

474
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:31,000
He becomes a contradiction to it.

475
00:32:33,000 --> 00:32:36,000
He chokes his fellow servant over a few thousand dollars.

476
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:40,000
He doesn't just forget the king's mercy.

477
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:44,000
He acts as if it never happened.

478
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:53,000
And Jesus says that's what it looks like when someone receives the grace of God but refuses to live in line with it.

479
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:58,000
It's what it looks like when forgiven people don't forgive.

480
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:01,000
It's not just wrong.

481
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,000
And it's absurd.

482
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:05,000
And it's unthinkable to the king.

483
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:08,000
And I think this is where it gets real for us.

484
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:14,000
This parable doesn't sit way out there in imaginary land.

485
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:18,000
Out there with some other cruel servant.

486
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:19,000
It's about us.

487
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:24,000
Any time we hoard grace instead of passing it on.

488
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:27,000
Let me finish with some last minute takeaways.

489
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:31,000
And each of these will probably hit you differently as the hardest.

490
00:33:31,000 --> 00:33:34,000
But I encourage you to take that weight to the king.

491
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:38,000
And give it to him and to your brother or sister.

492
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:42,000
One, forgiveness is not optional in the kingdom of God.

493
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:44,000
It's essential.

494
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:52,000
Not because it earns salvation but because it reveals that our salvation has truly taken root.

495
00:33:52,000 --> 00:34:01,000
